          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Iron primarily signifies strength, hardness, and crushing or unyielding power, with applications to messianic rule, oppressive power, and strong kingdoms; Daniel’s fourth kingdom is a recognized major instance.\",\"book_summary\":\"Iron signifies the crushing strength of Daniel’s fourth kingdom, identified by the book as Rome, the empire ruling when Christ came.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Strength, hardness, and crushing or unyielding power.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The crushing strength of Daniel’s fourth kingdom, specifically Rome.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus applies iron’s strength symbolism across several biblical contexts, whereas the book restricts the entry to Daniel’s fourth kingdom and its historical identification as Rome, excluding other prominent applications such as the messianic rod of iron, oppression, resilience, and stubbornness.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction at the center: Daniel’s crushing fourth kingdom is explicitly recognized within the consensus. The disagreement concerns whether that one historical application defines the entry’s scope.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus core of crushing strength but specifies one particular bearer of that quality: Daniel’s fourth kingdom, interpreted as Rome. Because this is a recognized consensus case rather than a replacement referent or an absent interpretation, the relationship is a substantive narrowing and therefore REFINED.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Daniel 2:40 strongly supports both the iron imagery and the crushing strength of the fourth kingdom, but the printed verses do not themselves identify that kingdom as Rome or establish the added chronological description that it ruled when Christ came. One could also argue for MATCH because the book’s minimum symbolic core, ‘crushing strength,’ is equivalent to the consensus core and the Roman identification is merely contextual detail.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
